docs/backlog.md:415:Until now the router ran one spinner chain (`jobTickMsg`) that broadcast a full `ChangedMsg` (snapshot and frame) to every held view at 10 Hz. The story asks for one tick that animates every row, blinking at 1 Hz, without rebroadcasting the snapshot. Decisions: (1) *One chain, renamed "animation"* (D2; formerly D5's "spinner chain"): `animationTickMsg{seq, at}`, `ensureTick`, `handleAnimationTick`, `animationState`. Only the router builds a `jobs.FrameMsg`; a source test enforces it. (2) *Two cadences:* 100 ms while `Running() > 0` (footer and row spinners need the frame), 500 ms when the view on screen implements `BlinkingView` and `WantsBlink()` is true and motion is blink. Otherwise no chain. (3) *The phase is read from the tick's own time:* `blinkOn = (UnixMilli/500) % 2 == 0`, so every row reads the same phase from the same tick. (4) *`FrameMsg{Frame, RenderedFrame, Blink}`* goes to the view on screen only, on every tick and on entry (`switchView`). `Frame` and `RenderedFrame` leave `jobs.ChangedMsg`, which keeps the snapshot only and now goes to every held view on a registry change. This reverses the earlier "one message and not two" comment, on purpose: the compiler then lists every reader of the frame, which protects against a view whose spinner freezes. (5) *Level signal:* `App.Update` is now a wrapper (`a.update` is today's switch, unchanged, then `ensureTick`). `ensureTick` runs after every message: it starts a chain when one is needed, replaces a slower chain by bumping `seq` (never two chains), and lets a live chain die at its next tick when nothing needs it. (6) *`tea.Every`, not `tea.Tick`:* `tea.Every` fires on the clock's boundaries, so `(UnixMilli/500) % 2` alternates strictly. With `tea.Tick` the drift accumulates and one blink is skipped (a steady 1 s `●`). (7) *Only the view on screen is asked `WantsBlink()`*, not every held view as the story's R3 said. Reason: the frame goes to the view on screen only, so a chain kept alive for an off-screen view would animate nothing. Stricter than the story; reversible in one line. (8) *`motionStatic` is a router field until A-02:* `setMotionStatic(bool)`, zero value = blink. A-02 wires it from `app.motion`; nothing is read from YAML here. `app.motion` does not exist yet (no `Motion` in `internal/config`). (9) *`datatable.Animating()` is not added here:* it has no caller before the first `BlinkingView` (A-09b/A-10a/A-13/A-14), and it goes with it. (10) *Four row-animation chains stay outside the router, declared as exceptions until A-16:* `oci_resources/busy.go` (`AdvanceSpinner`, A-09b), `containers/update.go` (`AdvanceSpinner`, A-14), `netdiag/ports_model.go` (`portsSpinnerCmd`, A-12), the OCI registry browser's `tagScanSpinnerIdx` (A-09a). A test lists them as declared exceptions: a new site fails it, and a stale exception fails it too. Criterion 4 of the story is therefore met on the router side here, and application-wide in A-16 (Q2, option A). (11) *Redraw-cost measurement is an in-process test, not a terminal capture* (Q1, option A): `TestMeasureRedrawCost` in `internal/app/redraw_measure_test.go`, skipped unless `DEVDESK_MEASURE_REDRAW=1`, runs a real `tea.Program` on the real router with `WithOutput(countingWriter)`, 120×40, for 10 s, and logs bytes per second for N = 0, 1, 20 blinking rows. The story named `TEA_TRACE`, which does not exist in Bubble Tea v1.3.10 (checked in the module source: no `TEA_TRACE`, no `os.Getenv`); the research document cited it from a page about another version. This measures what the renderer writes, not what a terminal shows over SSH; the real-SSH check stays with the A-06 checkpoint, where H2 is consigned. No view blinks before A-06, so the PR cannot show "20 blinking rows" in the real application. (12) *No ADR:* a lasting decision recorded here, per the project's convention. Hand-off: A-02 calls `setMotionStatic`; A-06 adds `SetBlinkPhase(msg.Blink)` to `:jobs`; the first `BlinkingView` adds `datatable.Animating()`; A-16 absorbs the declared exceptions.
